# .gitattributes -- line endings, and one file where they are load-bearing.
#
# `bootstrap/nen.sh` MUST check out with LF endings, on every platform, and this
# file is the only thing that makes that true. Git for Windows installs with
# `core.autocrlf=true` by DEFAULT, so without a rule here a Windows clone gets
# CRLF -- and bash then reads the shebang line's trailing `\r` as part of the
# interpreter path and dies with `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or
# directory`, or trips over `$'\r': command not found` on the first construct it
# meets. That is a total failure of the ONE file whose entire job is to run on a
# machine that has nothing else, and it fails in a way that looks like a corrupt
# download rather than a checkout setting.
#
# It is not hypothetical for this repository in particular: the maintainer's own
# host is Windows/Git Bash, §10 makes that a first-class supported platform, and
# `src/pipeline.test.ts` drives this script through `bash` on it.
#
# `* text=auto` first, so git normalizes to LF in the object database and leaves
# the working-tree spelling to the platform for everything else; then the
# explicit `eol=lf` for the shell file, which overrides autocrlf rather than
# merely asking it nicely.
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
